// Integer matrix product for the INT8 linear layers:
//   out[t][r] = sum_k in[t][k] * weight[r][k]      (int8 operands, exact int32 sums)
// Rows of `in` and `weight` hold `columns` contiguous bytes, read as 16-byte words. One
// workgroup computes a 128 x 256 tile; the caller pads tokens to a multiple of 128, rows to
// 256 and columns to 32, and keeps offsets multiples of 16 bytes.
//
// 32 subgroups of 16 lanes each own 32 tokens x 32 rows as eight 8 x 16 accumulators, the
// most that fit the register file without spilling. The operands advance 32 columns per step
// through double-buffered shared memory. Each step first requests the next step's operands
// from global memory, then multiplies, then stores what it requested: the multiply overlaps
// the wait for global memory.
//
// Measured on Intel Xe2 with Mesa (docs/kuno-rust-engine.md): the multiply instructions
// alone could run 2.2 times faster; what limits the kernel is copying operands from global
// memory, so callers should dispatch a few thousand weight rows at a time to keep the
// weight block cached while all tokens pass over it.
#version 460
#extension GL_GOOGLE_include_directive : require
#define SG 16
#define TM 8
#define MI 4
#define NJ 2
#define SPLIT 8
#include "gemm8.glsl"
